 "2026-10-28T14:49:15" :PRINCIPLES
 ("The agent loop is the atom: an LLM in a loop that reads state, takes an action via a tool (shell, file edit, message), observes the result, and repeats until a goal is met. Both Claude Code and OpenClaw are this loop wrapped in a 'harness' — strip away the UI and you find the same primitive."
  "Context is the scarce resource, not intelligence. Performance is dominated by what you put in the model's context window and when. This is why CLAUDE.md files, progress files, sub-agents (to compartmentalize context), and curated tool outputs matter more than clever prompting — the engineer's real job becomes context engineering."
  "Give the model general tools, not bespoke pipelines (the 'bitter lesson'). Claude Code rejected vector-DB indexing in favor of letting the model just run grep/ls agentically; OpenClaw plugs into the tools people already have (shell, email, browser, messaging). General capability + simple tools beats specialized scaffolding as models improve."
  "Keep the human as the director and the model as the execution layer. Default to asking permission before irreversible actions; the human supplies goals, standards, and architectural decisions while the agent supplies typing/execution. Autonomy is a dial the user controls, not a fixed setting."
  "Build for the model six months from now. Because capability is rising fast, the durable design choices are the unopinionated, low-level, scriptable ones that get *better automatically* as the underlying model improves — over-engineered scaffolding for today's weaknesses becomes dead weight tomorrow.")
 :SOURCES
 ((:TITLE "Claude Code: Best practices for agentic coding" :URL
   "https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/claude-code-best-practices" :BLURB
   "Anthropic released Claude Code as a deliberately low-level, unopinionated command-line tool that gives near-raw model access rather than forcing a workflow — the flexibility is the feature. The post codifies the core loop 'explore, plan, code, commit,' urging you to make the model research and form a strategy before it writes anything. Its single most-copied idea is the CLAUDE.md file: a special document Claude auto-loads into context holding your bash commands, code style, test instructions, and repo etiquette, so the agent starts every session pre-briefed. The throughline is that context engineering — tuning what the model sees — is the real skill, not prompt trickery."
   :AUTHOR "Boris Cherny / Anthropic Engineering" :KIND "essay")
  (:TITLE "Building Claude Code with Boris Cherny (The Pragmatic Engineer)" :URL
   "https://newsletter.pragmaticengineer.com/p/building-claude-code-with-boris-cherny" :BLURB
   "Cherny recounts how Claude Code began as a late-2024 prototyping side project that wrote 10–20% of his code, then climbed fast as Sonnet 3.5 → 4 → Opus improved, until he uninstalled his IDE entirely. Crucially, the team tried 'fancy' approaches to code search — local vector databases, recursive model-based indexing — and abandoned all of them for stale-index and permission reasons, instead letting the model agentically run plain commands like grep. The discussion goes deep on parallel sub-agents, PR structure, deterministic review patterns, and retrieving context from large codebases. The mental model: Claude Code turns the LLM into a persistent agent that reads files, runs commands, edits code, and spawns sub-agents inside your repo."
   :AUTHOR "Gergely Orosz with Boris Cherny" :KIND "talk")
  (:TITLE "OpenClaw (Wikipedia overview + project origin as Warelay)" :URL
   "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenClaw" :BLURB
   "OpenClaw is a free, open-source autonomous AI agent created by Austrian developer Peter Steinberger (ex-PSPDFKit), first released November 2025 as 'Warelay' and renamed several times before settling on OpenClaw. Unlike cloud agents, it is self-hosted — it runs entirely on the user's own hardware, is LLM-provider-agnostic, and uses everyday messaging apps (WhatsApp, iMessage, Slack, Discord) as its primary interface. It connects to email, calendars, browsers, and a local shell so agents can run commands, manage files, and automate web tasks, letting non-programmers build real automated businesses. It became the fastest project in GitHub history to cross ~300k stars, and Steinberger later joined OpenAI while moving the project to an independent foundation to keep it open source."
   :AUTHOR "Peter Steinberger / OpenClaw Foundation" :KIND "other")
  (:TITLE "How I Created OpenClaw, the Breakthrough AI Agent (TED talk transcript)" :URL
   "https://singjupost.com/how-i-created-openclaw-the-breakthrough-ai-agent-peter-steinberger-transcript/"
   :BLURB
   "In his own words, Steinberger traces OpenClaw's evolution from a simple WhatsApp bot into a global open-source phenomenon, describing an agent that 'improvises' — given general tools and a goal, it figures out the steps itself rather than following hard-coded flows. He highlights how the framework let non-programmers build surprisingly complex real-world operations (an automated brewery, tutoring services) because the agent bridges natural-language intent and tool execution. The talk frames the project's mission as world-changing access to automation rather than company-building, foreshadowing his choice to hand OpenClaw to a foundation. It is the clearest statement of why a messaging-first, self-hosted agent resonated so widely."
   :AUTHOR "Peter Steinberger" :KIND "talk")
  (:TITLE "Effective harnesses for long-running agents" :URL
   "https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-harnesses-for-long-running-agents" :BLURB
   "This post tackles the central problem of long-running agents: how an agent regains situational awareness when starting from a fresh, empty context window. Its key technique is durable external memory — e.g., a progress file (claude-progress.txt) kept alongside git history — so the agent can reconstruct 'where am I and what's left' the way a good engineer reorients each morning. It treats the harness (memory files, tool design, the action loop) as the real engineering surface rather than the model weights themselves. This is the conceptual bridge explaining why both coding agents and personal agents converge on similar context-and-memory architectures."
   :AUTHOR "Anthropic Engineering" :KIND "essay")))
