 ("An agent is a function that maps a percept sequence (observations from an environment) to actions. Intelligence is not magic — it is the disciplined selection of the action that maximizes expected success given what has been perceived. This 'rational agent' formulation (Russell & Norvig) is the bedrock from which every modern LLM agent is just a specific instantiation."
  "Reasoning and acting must be interleaved, not separated. Pure reasoning (chain-of-thought) is ungrounded and hallucinates; pure acting is blind. The breakthrough of ReAct is the loop: the agent articulates WHY (reason), takes an action, observes the result, and updates its plan — grounding thought in feedback from the world."
  "The minimal anatomy of an LLM agent is: LLM (the brain/policy) + Planning (task decomposition & self-reflection) + Memory (short-term in-context + long-term external retrieval) + Tool Use (calling external APIs to overcome the model's frozen weights). Most agent designs are recombinations of these four parts."
  "Tools extend the model beyond what its weights can do — arithmetic, current facts, code execution, proprietary data. Crucially, the model itself should decide when, which, and how to call a tool, because what a model finds useful differs from what a human assumes is useful (Toolformer). Capability comes from grounding the model in external systems, not from the model alone."
  "Simplicity beats abstraction. The most reliable agentic systems are built from composable patterns (prompt chaining, routing, parallelization, orchestrator-workers), and you should only add agentic autonomy when the task genuinely requires open-ended, unpredictable steps. Autonomy buys flexibility at the cost of latency, expense, and compounding errors — so 'an LLM using tools in a loop' should be reached for last, not first (Anthropic).")
 :SOURCES
 ((:TITLE "Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach — Chapter 2: Intelligent Agents" :URL
   "https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~russell/aima1e/chapter02.pdf" :BLURB
   "Russell & Norvig frame the whole of AI around one unifying abstraction: the agent — anything that perceives its environment through sensors and acts upon it through actuators. They define an agent formally as a function mapping percept sequences to actions, and define a RATIONAL agent as one that selects the action expected to maximize its performance measure given its percepts and knowledge. The chapter builds a ladder of architectures — simple reflex, model-based, goal-based, utility-based, and learning agents — that maps almost perfectly onto how people reason about LLM agents today. It also introduces bounded/limited rationality (after Herbert Simon): perfect optimal action is computationally infeasible, so good agents act appropriately under resource constraints. Read this first and every later agent paper becomes a special case."
   :AUTHOR "Stuart Russell & Peter Norvig" :KIND "book")
  (:TITLE "ReAct: Synergizing Reasoning and Acting in Language Models" :URL
   "https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03629" :BLURB
   "Yao et al. observe that LLM reasoning (chain-of-thought) and acting (generating tool/API calls) had been studied separately, and show that interleaving them produces a powerful synergy. In ReAct, the model alternates between verbal reasoning traces ('Thought: I should look up X') and concrete actions ('Action: search[X]'), then folds the resulting observation back into its next reasoning step. Reasoning lets the agent create, track, and revise plans; acting lets it pull real information from the environment to correct itself — directly attacking the hallucination and error-propagation that plague pure chain-of-thought. The approach beats reasoning-only and acting-only baselines across HotpotQA, FEVER, ALFWorld, and WebShop, while also being far more interpretable because you can read the agent's thoughts. This interleaved loop is the conceptual heart of agentic engineering."
   :AUTHOR
   "Shunyu Yao, Jeffrey Zhao, Dian Yu, Nan Du, Izhak Shafran, Karthik Narasimhan, Yuan Cao (Princeton & Google)"
   :KIND "paper")
  (:TITLE "Toolformer: Language Models Can Teach Themselves to Use Tools" :URL
   "https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.04761" :BLURB
   "Schick et al. start from a paradox: large LLMs can solve novel tasks from a few examples yet fail at trivial things like arithmetic and factual lookup that tiny specialized systems nail. Their fix is to teach the model to call external tools — a calculator, search engines, a Q&A system, a translator, a calendar — embedding API calls inline in text. The elegant part is the self-supervised training: the model proposes candidate API calls at various positions, then KEEPS only the calls that actually reduce its loss in predicting subsequent tokens, learning tool use from its own predictions with just a handful of demonstrations per tool. A key design principle: the model should decide for itself when and which tool to use, because what a model finds useful may differ from what a human assumes. The result is a model that gains substantially better zero-shot performance while staying fully general."
   :AUTHOR "Timo Schick, Jane Dwivedi-Yu, Roberto Dessì, et al. (Meta AI)" :KIND "paper")
  (:TITLE "LLM Powered Autonomous Agents" :URL
   "https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2023-06-23-agent/" :BLURB
   "Weng distills the explosion of 2023 agent work (AutoGPT, BabyAGI, GPT-Engineer) into one clean architecture where the LLM acts as the agent's brain, surrounded by three components: Planning, Memory, and Tool Use. Planning covers task decomposition (Chain of Thought, Tree of Thoughts) and self-reflection, where the agent critiques its past actions to improve future ones (citing ReAct, Reflexion, and self-refinement). Memory is split into short-term (in-context learning within the prompt window) and long-term (external vector stores with fast retrieval for effectively unlimited recall). Tool use lets the agent call external APIs for current information, code execution, and proprietary data the weights can't hold. It's the best map of the whole territory — a structured reading list and conceptual framework in one."
   :AUTHOR "Lilian Weng (Lil'Log)" :KIND "essay")
  (:TITLE "Building Effective Agents" :URL
   "https://www.anthropic.com/research/building-effective-agents" :BLURB
   "Anthropic's core argument cuts against hype: the most successful agentic systems are built from simple, composable patterns, not heavyweight frameworks and abstractions. They draw a crucial line between WORKFLOWS (LLMs orchestrated through predefined code paths) and AGENTS (systems where the LLM dynamically directs its own process and tool use) — later distilled to a tight definition: agents are 'LLMs autonomously using tools in a loop.' They catalog the building blocks engineers actually need — prompt chaining, routing, parallelization, orchestrator-workers, and evaluator-optimizer — and advise reaching for full agentic autonomy only for open-ended problems where you can't hardcode the steps. The piece is candid about tradeoffs: autonomy raises cost, latency, and the risk of compounding errors, so it demands sandboxing, guardrails, measurement, and iteration. The throughline is engineering discipline: start simple, add complexity only when it demonstrably earns its keep."
   :AUTHOR "Anthropic (Engineering)" :KIND "essay")))
(:REF "library/large-language-models" :MAP "library" :NODE-ID "large-language-models" :AT
