 ("Language modeling is next-token prediction: an LLM is trained to estimate the probability of the next token given prior tokens. All capabilities — translation, reasoning, coding, dialogue — emerge as side effects of compressing text well enough to predict it. Master this objective and most of the field re-derives from it."
  "Attention is the core computational primitive. Instead of processing sequences step-by-step (recurrence) or locally (convolution), self-attention lets every token directly weigh its relevance to every other token in parallel. This both removes the sequential bottleneck (enabling massive parallel training) and lets the model route information across arbitrary distances."
  "Capability scales predictably with compute, parameters, and data via power laws. Loss decreases smoothly as a power-law function of model size (N), dataset size (D), and compute (C). Because the curve is predictable, you can plan a model's performance before training it — and there exists a compute-optimal allocation between making models bigger vs. feeding them more data."
  "Scale produces qualitative, not just quantitative, change: in-context (few-shot) learning. A large enough model can perform a new task from a few examples placed in its prompt — with no weight updates. The model effectively 'learns' at inference time, turning a static predictor into a general-purpose, promptable task solver."
  "Pretraining yields raw capability; alignment yields usefulness. A base model trained only to predict text is not inherently helpful, truthful, or safe. Fine-tuning on human demonstrations and preferences (RLHF) aligns outputs with human intent — and a small aligned model can be preferred over a 100x larger unaligned one, proving alignment is a distinct axis from raw scale.")
 :SOURCES
 ((:TITLE "Attention Is All You Need" :URL "https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762" :BLURB
   "This 2017 paper introduced the Transformer, the architecture underlying every modern LLM. Its central move was to discard recurrence and convolution entirely and build the model purely on self-attention, where each token computes a weighted relevance score against all other tokens via scaled dot-product attention, run in parallel across multiple 'heads.' Because there is no sequential dependency, training parallelizes across the whole sequence, making it far faster to train at scale, while attention captures long-range dependencies that RNNs struggled with. It also introduced positional encodings to inject word order and the encoder-decoder structure now ubiquitous in NLP. Read it to understand the literal mechanism inside every LLM."
   :AUTHOR
   "Ashish Vaswani, Noam Shazeer, Niki Parmar, Jakob Uszkoreit, Llion Jones, Aidan N. Gomez, Łukasz Kaiser, Illia Polosukhin (Google)"
   :KIND "paper")
  (:TITLE "Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models" :URL "https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08361" :BLURB
   "This 2020 paper established that language model performance is not erratic but governed by smooth power laws. Cross-entropy loss falls predictably as a power-law function of three things — number of parameters, dataset size, and training compute — with trends holding across more than seven orders of magnitude. Crucially, architectural details like depth vs. width matter little within broad ranges; scale dominates. The authors showed larger models are more sample-efficient, so the compute-optimal strategy is to train very large models on modest data and stop before convergence. This is the intellectual license for the scaling race, later refined by the Chinchilla (Hoffmann et al., 2022) compute-optimal results."
   :AUTHOR "Jared Kaplan, Sam McCandlish, et al. (OpenAI)" :KIND "paper")
  (:TITLE "Language Models are Few-Shot Learners (GPT-3)" :URL "https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.14165"
   :BLURB
   "This 2020 paper introduced GPT-3, a 175-billion-parameter autoregressive model, and showed that scaling alone unlocks a new capability: in-context learning. Without any gradient updates, GPT-3 could perform tasks from a handful of examples placed directly in its prompt — few-shot, one-shot, or even zero-shot. It reached competitive performance across dozens of NLP benchmarks plus novel on-the-fly tasks like arithmetic and using made-up words in sentences. The work demonstrated that a single general model could replace many task-specific fine-tuned models, and candidly documented limitations and societal risks (including human-indistinguishable generated text). This is the empirical proof that scale yields qualitatively new behavior."
   :AUTHOR "Tom B. Brown, Benjamin Mann, Nick Ryder, et al. (OpenAI)" :KIND "paper")
  (:TITLE "Training Language Models to Follow Instructions with Human Feedback (InstructGPT)" :URL
   "https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02155" :BLURB
   "This 2022 paper confronts the gap between capability and usefulness: bigger models are not automatically more aligned with what users actually want, and can be untruthful, toxic, or unhelpful. It lays out the now-standard three-step RLHF pipeline: (1) supervised fine-tuning on human-written demonstrations, (2) training a reward model from human rankings of model outputs, and (3) optimizing the policy against that reward model using PPO. The headline result is striking — a 1.3B-parameter InstructGPT was preferred by humans over the 175B GPT-3, a 100x size gap closed by alignment alone. It also improved truthfulness and reduced toxicity with minimal capability loss, establishing alignment as a separate, high-leverage axis from raw scale."
   :AUTHOR "Long Ouyang, Jeff Wu, Ryan Lowe, et al. (OpenAI)" :KIND "paper")))
(:REF "library/stripe-payment-processor" :MAP "library" :NODE-ID "stripe-payment-processor" :AT
