 ("Networks fail in unpredictable ways, so any API that mutates state with money attached must be idempotent — the same operation invoked any number of times must produce the same side effect exactly once. Idempotency keys are the canonical mechanism: client generates a unique ID, server stores the result and replays it for retries."
  "A payment processor is a distributed system spanning at minimum the merchant's server and Stripe's API, plus card networks and banks downstream. Every request crosses trust and reliability boundaries, so design for failure at every hop (retry with exponential backoff + jitter, atomic phases, completer processes for foreign state mutations)."
  "An API is infrastructure with a permanent contract. Once even one user integrates, breaking changes are extraordinarily expensive — so versioning, backwards compatibility, and getting the design right the first time are first-order engineering concerns, not afterthoughts."
  "The product is the developer experience. Stripe's growth came from making a deeply complex domain (card networks, fraud, compliance, currencies, settlement) feel like seven lines of code. Craftsmanship in APIs, docs, and abstractions compounds because vastly more people consume the interface than produce it."
  "Money systems demand operational excellence: people's livelihoods depend on the system working. This drives a culture of writing things down, canonical log lines, online (zero-downtime) migrations via dual writes, and treating reliability as a contract that must be re-earned every request.")
 :SOURCES
 ((:TITLE "Designing robust and predictable APIs with idempotency" :URL
   "https://stripe.com/blog/idempotency" :BLURB
   "Stripe's API is itself a distributed system: at minimum your server talking to theirs over an unreliable network where connections drop, requests get half-processed, and confirmations get lost. The fix is to make endpoints idempotent — clients send a unique key with each mutating request, and the server stores the result of the first execution and replays it on retries, guaranteeing side effects (like a charge) happen exactly once. Pair this with consistent failure handling, exponential backoff with jitter, and you can drive any complex integration to a consistent state despite network chaos. The post lays out the core principles that have become canon for any developer building serious payment or financial APIs."
   :AUTHOR "Brandur Leach (Stripe)" :KIND "essay")
  (:TITLE "APIs as infrastructure: future-proofing Stripe with versioning" :URL
   "https://stripe.com/blog/api-versioning" :BLURB
   "Once even one developer integrates against your API, your interface is a contract you can never casually break. Stripe rejects the common 'v1/v2/v3' URL-prefix versioning because it forces painful re-integrations and traps users on old versions. Instead, every Stripe account is pinned to the API version active when it was created, and the codebase contains version-gated transformation layers that translate between old and new shapes. The essay frames APIs as infrastructure — like Unix system calls — where the right answer is to design carefully up front and treat each new version as expensive technical debt to be avoided when possible."
   :AUTHOR "Brandur Leach (Stripe)" :KIND "essay")
  (:TITLE "Online migrations at scale" :URL "https://stripe.com/blog/online-migrations" :BLURB
   "Stripe can never take a maintenance window — businesses are always transacting — so when its data models need to change underneath hundreds of millions of live Subscription objects, it uses a four-step dual-writing pattern: (1) write to both old and new tables, (2) backfill old data into the new table, (3) verify and switch reads to the new table (using tools like GitHub's Scientist to compare results), (4) reverse writes so new is primary and old is archive, then drop the old. The post is a clinic in incremental, reversible change management for production databases. It's the canonical playbook for any team facing a 'we have to migrate millions of rows but the system has to stay up' problem."
   :AUTHOR "Jacqueline Xu (Stripe)" :KIND "essay")
  (:TITLE "Implementing Stripe-like Idempotency Keys in Postgres" :URL
   "https://brandur.org/idempotency-keys" :BLURB
   "Building on the public Stripe post, Brandur walks through a reference implementation (the 'Rocket Rides' demo app) showing how to make a payment endpoint truly idempotent end-to-end. The key insight: separate local state mutations (which an ACID database can roll back) from foreign state mutations (a Stripe charge, an email send) which cannot be undone. Group work into restartable atomic phases keyed by an idempotency key, and use a background completer process to drive any half-finished request to completion even if the original server died. The post shows why a naive 'just save the response' approach is insufficient and gives concrete Postgres schema and code patterns."
   :AUTHOR "Brandur Leach" :KIND "essay")
  (:TITLE "CS183C Session 11: Patrick Collison, Stripe (Stanford Blitzscaling)" :URL
   "https://medium.com/cs183c-blitzscaling-class-collection/cs183c-session-11-patrick-collison-stripe-84057f358ecb"
   :BLURB
   "Patrick Collison tells the founding story directly: he and his brother John were building iPhone apps to pay for college and noticed that while Slicehost had radically lowered the activation energy for spinning up a server, nothing comparable existed for accepting payments online — so they set out to build 'Slicehost for payments.' He discusses Stripe's deliberately conventional org structure, the CEO's job reduced to strategy + culture + senior hiring (per Ali Rowghani), and his deep skepticism of founding mythology. He also notes Alan Kay's barb that computer science is pop culture — a Brownian motion through the problem space — and argues that reading history is 'a way to cheat' because most solutions are already written down. This worldview (rigor, craftsmanship, long abstractions, reading widely) is the cultural source code that explains why Stripe's engineering blog reads the way it does."
   :AUTHOR "Patrick Collison (interviewed by John Lilly)" :KIND "talk")))
(:REF "library/databases" :MAP "library" :NODE-ID "databases" :AT "2026-10-28T14:49:15" :PRINCIPLES
 ("Data independence: applications and queries should be insulated from how data is physically stored, indexed, or laid out — change the storage, the queries still work."
  "The relational model: data is best represented as time-varying sets of n-ary relations (tables of tuples), manipulated by a closed algebra/calculus, and queried declaratively rather than navigationally."
  "Normalization eliminates redundancy and update anomalies by decomposing relations so that every non-key fact depends on the key, the whole key, and nothing but the key — replacing pointers with shared key values."
  "ACID transactions: a unit of work must be Atomic (all-or-nothing), Consistency-preserving, Isolated from concurrent work (serializable, typically via two-phase locking), and Durable across failures (typically via write-ahead logging)."
  "A query optimizer can beat hand-written procedural data access: given statistics and a cost model, it enumerates access paths and join orders and picks a plan, which is what makes declarative SQL practical.")
 :SOURCES
 ((:TITLE "A Relational Model of Data for Large Shared Data Banks" :URL
   "https://www.seas.upenn.edu/~zives/03f/cis550/codd.pdf" :BLURB
   "Codd argues that future users of large data banks must be insulated from how the data is physically stored — what he calls 'data independence.' He proposes representing data as n-ary mathematical relations (tables of tuples) instead of the tree- and network-structured files (IMS, CODASYL) then in vogue, which forced programs to navigate pointer chains and broke whenever the storage layout changed. He introduces normalization (turning nested/non-simple domains into flat relations linked by primary keys) to eliminate pointers and update anomalies, and sketches a 'universal data sublanguage' — the seed that became SQL. The paper also lays out a relational algebra/calculus to reason about queries mathematically, making query optimization possible. Read this and 90% of the database literature suddenly makes sense."
   :AUTHOR "E. F. Codd" :KIND "paper")
  (:TITLE "The Transaction Concept: Virtues and Limitations" :URL
   "https://jimgray.azurewebsites.net/papers/thetransactionconcept.pdf" :BLURB
   "Gray defines a transaction as a group of actions that takes the database from one consistent state to another, and must either fully commit or leave no trace (atomicity + durability). He shows that transactions are the right structuring primitive not just for databases but for reliable distributed systems generally. The paper develops the implementation toolkit: write-ahead logging for atomicity and durability, two-phase locking for isolation (he proved that well-formed two-phase transactions are serializable), and recovery via UNDO/REDO over the log. It also honestly catalogs the limits — long-running transactions, lower isolation levels, sub-transactions — that motivate decades of follow-on research."
   :AUTHOR "Jim Gray" :KIND "paper")
  (:TITLE "Access Path Selection in a Relational Database Management System" :URL
   "https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~brewer/cs262/3-selinger79.pdf" :BLURB
   "In SQL, users state what they want, not how to get it; this paper shows how a system can mechanically choose the 'how.' The optimizer enumerates access paths (full scan vs. index scan), considers join algorithms (nested loop, sort-merge), and uses table/column statistics plus a simple cost model (page fetches + W × CPU calls) to pick the cheapest plan. The key trick: dynamic programming over join orders combined with the idea of 'interesting orders' (an ordering produced as a side effect that helps a later operator) prunes the n! search space down to something tractable. The quiet but enormous result is that declarative query languages can match or beat hand-written procedural data access, which is what made SQL viable as a productivity language."
   :AUTHOR "P. Selinger, M. Astrahan, D. Chamberlin, R. Lorie, T. Price" :KIND "paper")
  (:TITLE "Architecture of a Database System" :URL
   "https://dsf.berkeley.edu/papers/fntdb07-architecture.pdf" :BLURB
   "A book-length tour of the systems-design issues that make a real DBMS work: process models (thread-per-connection vs. process pools), parallel architectures (shared-memory, shared-disk, shared-nothing), the storage manager (buffer pool, B+-trees, latches), the transaction system (locking, logging, ARIES-style recovery), and the query processor (parser → optimizer → iterator-based executor). It explicitly compares how Oracle, DB2, SQL Server, Postgres, and MySQL solve each problem differently, so you learn the design space rather than one product. After Codd, Gray, and Selinger give you the ideas, this paper shows you how those ideas actually compose into a running system."
   :AUTHOR "Joseph M. Hellerstein, Michael Stonebraker, James Hamilton" :KIND "paper")
  (:TITLE "What Goes Around Comes Around" :URL
   "https://people.cs.umass.edu/~yanlei/courses/CS691LL-f06/papers/SH05.pdf" :BLURB
   "A 35-year tour through nine eras of data models — hierarchical (IMS), network (CODASYL), relational, entity-relationship, semantic, object-oriented, object-relational, semi-structured, XML — showing that almost every 'new' model is a thinly disguised return to a previously-failed idea. The authors distill the recurring lessons: physical and logical data independence are non-negotiable; tree-structured models are too restrictive; high-level set-at-a-time queries beat record-at-a-time navigation; complexity kills adoption (this is why CODASYL and XML databases lost). The piece is opinionated, witty, and saves you from believing the next round of NoSQL/graph/document hype on its own marketing terms. Read it after Codd to understand why the relational model won and keeps winning."
   :AUTHOR "Michael Stonebraker, Joseph M. Hellerstein" :KIND "essay")))
